====================== GUIDELINES ======================
0. Principle: Never redeclare an existing symbol or datatype. Output pure SMT-LIB only. End every declaration and every assert with a single-line comment that quotes the exact words of the requirement.

1. Declarations:
  1.1 Datatypes: 
    - Use one (declare-datatypes) per sort. 
    - Constructors must be flat and appear once only. 
    - Place each constructor on its own line, followed by a JSON comment of the form ; {"attr":"...","entity":"...","true":"...","src":"..."} 
    - Use enums only when exactly one label can be true at a time. 
    - When several labels may all hold, declare separate Boolean flags or a unary predicate. 
    - If a requirement constrains that enum, first declare a value constant of the enum sort. 
  Example:
    (declare-datatypes () ((sex male female)))
    (declare-const patient_sex sex)
    (assert (or (= patient_sex male) (= patient_sex female))) ; "male or female"
  1.2 Constants:
    - Declare with (declare-const name sort).
    - Bool constants must end with a comment containing keys attr, entity, true, false, src.
    - Numeric Int or Real constants must end with a comment containing keys attr, entity, unit, src.
  1.3 Data-efficient encoding
    - Prefer the raw numeric, date, or enum value and assert a comparison such as 
          (declare-const hemoglobin_value Real)
          (assert (>= hemoglobin_value 10.0)) ; "hemoglobin ≥ 10 g/dL"
    - Add a Boolean flag only if the raw value is unavailable or already supplied upstream.

2. Encoding and use
  2.1 Enums:
    - Always compare a declared constant to exactly one constructor, for example (= patient_sex male).
    - If overlap is possible switch to Boolean flags or predicates; never write two equalities on the same enum constant.
  2.2 Boolean logic
    - "and", "or", and "=>" accept Bool arguments only.

2.3 Derived flags
When a Boolean merely mirrors an enum value write it as a definitional equality, for example
(assert (= is_bone_only_cohort (= diagnosis bone_only_urothelial))) ; "bone-only cohort"

3. Assertions and policies
  3.1 Assert every clause that contains must or required.
  3.2 If an and or or chain is long, wrap it in a zero-argument define-fun.
  3.3 Remove redundant tests and tautologies. Ensure every constructor comparison uses a declared constant of the same sort.

4. Validation checklist (implicit)
  - No duplicate names.
  - Every constructor comparison uses the correct enum sort.
  - No constant is equated to two different constructors.
  - Units match in numeric comparisons.

These points are exhaustive; no other formatting or prose is permitted.


========================= STRICT RULES =========================
1. Never redeclare a symbol or datatype that already exists.
2. If no new symbols are needed, include the variableaddition block with only its header line.
3. The varsemantics block must be a single line of minified JSON listing only the symbols declared in variableaddition.
4. Produce only SMT-LIB and inline comments beginning with a semicolon; add no other prose.
5. Inline comments must quote the requirement text verbatim, without paraphrase.
6. If a requirement would force one enum-typed constant to equal two different constructors, abandon enum equality and re-encode with Boolean flags.


============= TRANSLATION PROCESS (to run inside the <think> block) =============
1. Collect every symbol that already appears in <partial_smt_program>.
2. Decide whether the field is exclusive (enum equality) or overlapping (Boolean flag).
3. List any extra symbols you must introduce, borrowing wording from <contextual_text> or <source_document>.
4. Apply data-efficient encoding:
  - declare the raw numeric or enum value whenever the text constrains a scalar.
  - fall back to a Boolean flag only when the raw value is missing or already fixed upstream.
5. Declare each missing symbol on its own line, ending with an inline motivation comment.
6. Translate the requirement into one or more (assert …) statements that use only declared symbols.
7. Verify in the chain that
  - no new declaration duplicates an existing symbol, and
  - every symbol used in the assertions has been declared.


